  model, and it's the model because the two pure forms each fail badly.

  [1mOn "change":[0m notifying on every field write is the fastest way to get the bell ignored — a typo fix in a description
  shouldn't ping four people. I'd recommend a [1mnamed set of significant events[0m (status change, assignee change, deletion,
  comment) rather than diffing all fields. Naming them also makes the notification text writable: "Alex assigned you
  [3mFix login[0m" needs to know [3mwhich[0m kind of change happened, which a generic field-diff can't tell you.

  One rule I'd build in regardless: [1mnever notify you of your own action.[0m It's the single most common complaint about
  notification systems and it's much easier to enforce from the start than to retrofit.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What makes a user 'care about' a task? → Implicit + manual override (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246m· What counts as a change worth notifying about? → Named significant events (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Two setup questions, then I'll present the design.

  Since this is greenfield, the stack is a real choice rather than a given. The repo today is a no-build static page,
  and I'd like to preserve that simplicity where it doesn't cost correctness — the interesting part of this project is
  the notification routing, not the toolchain.

  And per good practice on a new project: tooling is cheapest to set up before any code exists, so it's worth deciding
  now rather than retrofitting.
